{{!
    Marketing attribution capture for the Friends with Habits subdomain.

    WHY THIS EXISTS AS INLINE JS RATHER THAN AN IMPORT
    habits.therr.com is Handlebars SSR and ships no React bundle, so
    therr-react's captureAttribution() - the function that does exactly this on
    www.therr.com, wired up in components/Layout.tsx - never runs on this host.
    Until this partial existed, habits.therr.com could take a fully tagged
    ?utm_campaign= click and write nothing anywhere: main."userAcquisition"
    received nothing from this domain, so the paid "measured arm" measured
    nothing and every habits signup looked organic.

    THIS MUST STAY IN LOCKSTEP WITH
      therr-public-library/therr-react/src/utilities/attribution.ts
    Same storage key, same IUserAcquisition field names, same first-touch rule,
    same truncation caps, same self-referral exclusion. The server sanitizer
    (UserAcquisitionStore.sanitizeUserAcquisition) drops unknown keys silently,
    so a field renamed on one side and not the other does not error - it just
    stops being recorded. If you change either file, change both.

    Loaded from habitsAnalytics.hbs, which every habits view already includes,
    so capture happens on the FIRST page of the session whatever page that is -
    the landing page, a blog post, or an /invite/:userName link. Capturing only
    on the registration page would attribute every signup to the last click
    before the form instead of the one that brought the visitor in.

    Storage is sessionStorage, matching attribution.ts: attribution that
    outlives the visit would credit a months-old campaign for an unrelated
    signup. Every access is wrapped - private browsing throws on access, not on
    read, and telemetry must never be what breaks a page.

    Two constraints inherited from habitsAnalytics.hbs, both of which took down
    every habits page when they were violated:
      1. No partial call inside this comment. Handlebars executes one even
         inside an HTML comment.
      2. No long-form comment delimiter inside this block. The long form ends at
         the FIRST closing delimiter, even one written as prose.
}}
<script>
    (function () {
        var KEY = 'therrUserAcquisition';
        var MAX_UTM = 255;
        var MAX_REFERRER = 1024;
        var MAX_PATH = 512;

        function truncate(value, max) {
            if (!value) { return undefined; }
            var trimmed = String(value).trim();
            return trimmed ? trimmed.slice(0, max) : undefined;
        }

        // A referrer from one of our own properties is the cross-domain hop the
        // funnel is made of, not an acquisition source. Recording it would
        // overwrite "arrived from Google" with "arrived from therr.app".
        function isSelfReferral(referrer) {
            try {
                return /(^|\.)therr\.(com|app)$/i.test(new URL(referrer).hostname);
            } catch (e) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        try {
            if (!window.sessionStorage) { return; }
            // First touch wins, and the rule is the PRESENCE of a record, not
            // whether that record carried a campaign: a session that started
            // direct and later picked up a utm_* from an internal link was
            // still acquired directly.
            if (window.sessionStorage.getItem(KEY)) { return; }

            var params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search || '');
            var acquisition = { surface: 'habits' };
            var utmKeys = [
                ['utmSource', 'utm_source'],
                ['utmMedium', 'utm_medium'],
                ['utmCampaign', 'utm_campaign'],
                ['utmContent', 'utm_content'],
                ['utmTerm', 'utm_term']
            ];
            utmKeys.forEach(function (pair) {
                var value = truncate(params.get(pair[1]), MAX_UTM);
                if (value) { acquisition[pair[0]] = value; }
            });

            var referrer = truncate(document.referrer, MAX_REFERRER);
            if (referrer && !isSelfReferral(referrer)) { acquisition.referrer = referrer; }

            // Path only, never the query string - it carries the UTMs already
            // and anything else on it is not ours to store.
            acquisition.landingPath = truncate(window.location.pathname, MAX_PATH);

            window.sessionStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(acquisition));
        } catch (e) { /* storage disabled or blocked - attribution is advisory */ }
    })();
</script>
{{!
    Carry the session's attribution across the Play Store hop.

    Most visitors leave this site through a store button, not /register: in the
    30 days to 18 Oct 2026, 16 store clicks against 1 register_start and 0
    sign_up. A bare store link drops the visitor's UTMs at Play, so the install
    reads as google-play / organic and the click that paid for it is lost.

    Play hands whatever is in the link's referrer parameter to the installed
    app, where TherrMobile's installReferrer.ts parses it into the registration's
    userAcquisition record, and where GA4 reads it as the app user's first-touch
    source. So every habits store link gets the session's first-touch UTMs as its
    referrer. An untagged visit is marked habits.therr.com / referral, with the
    external referring host in utm_content when there is one, so a web-driven
    install stays distinguishable from Play's own placeholder, which the app
    deliberately discards. A visit with no campaign of its own also gets the
    page type it landed on as utm_campaign (web-invite, web-challenge,
    web-guide, web-landing and so on), so friend invites and the challenge
    guides can be read apart.

    A separate script block from the capture above on purpose: the capture
    test runs that block with document shadowed down to document.referrer, and
    this one needs the real DOM.
}}
<script>
    (function () {
        var STORE_URL = /^https:\/\/play\.google\.com\/store\/apps\/details\?id=com\.therr\.habits(&|$)/;
        var utmKeys = [
            ['utmSource', 'utm_source'],
            ['utmMedium', 'utm_medium'],
            ['utmCampaign', 'utm_campaign'],
            ['utmContent', 'utm_content'],
            ['utmTerm', 'utm_term']
        ];

        function storeReferrer() {
            var stored = {};
            try {
                stored = JSON.parse(window.sessionStorage.getItem('therrUserAcquisition') || '{}') || {};
            } catch (e) { /* no record - still mark the install as web-driven */ }

            var params = new URLSearchParams();
            utmKeys.forEach(function (pair) {
                if (stored[pair[0]]) { params.set(pair[1], stored[pair[0]]); }
            });
            if (!params.toString() && stored.referrer) {
                try {
                    params.set('utm_content', new URL(stored.referrer).hostname.replace(/^www\./, ''));
                } catch (e) { /* unparseable referrer - leave it out */ }
            }
            if (!params.get('utm_source')) { params.set('utm_source', 'habits.therr.com'); }
            if (!params.get('utm_medium')) { params.set('utm_medium', 'referral'); }
            if (!params.get('utm_campaign')) {
                params.set('utm_campaign', surfaceCampaign(stored.landingPath || window.location.pathname));
            }
            return params.toString();
        }

        // An untagged visit's install still says which page brought it: an invite
        // link a friend sent, a challenge link, a challenge guide, the landing page.
        // Without this every one of them read as the same habits.therr.com /
        // referral, so the invite loop and the guides could not be told apart in
        // GA4 (firstUserCampaignName) or main."userAcquisition"."utmCampaign".
        // A tagged visit keeps its own campaign: this only fills a blank.
        function surfaceCampaign(path) {
            var p = String(path || '/');
            if (/^\/invite\//.test(p)) { return 'web-invite'; }
            if (/^\/claim-pact\//.test(p)) { return 'web-pact-invite'; }
            if (/^\/c\//.test(p)) { return 'web-challenge'; }
            if (/^\/(challenges|es\/retos|fr\/defis)(\/|$)/.test(p)) { return 'web-guide'; }
            if (/^\/coaches(\/|$)/.test(p)) { return 'web-coaches'; }
            if (/^\/blog(\/|$)/.test(p)) { return 'web-blog'; }
            if (/^\/u\//.test(p)) { return 'web-profile'; }
            if (/^\/(es|fr)?\/?$/.test(p)) { return 'web-landing'; }
            return 'web-other';
        }

        function decorate(anchor) {
            var href = anchor && anchor.getAttribute && anchor.getAttribute('href');
            if (!href || !STORE_URL.test(href) || /[?&]referrer=/.test(href)) { return; }
            anchor.setAttribute('href', href + '&referrer=' + encodeURIComponent(storeReferrer()));
        }

        function decorateAll() {
            try {
                Array.prototype.forEach.call(document.querySelectorAll('a[href]'), decorate);
            } catch (e) { /* a broken link decorator must never break the store link */ }
        }

        // This partial loads in <head>, before the links exist. The click
        // listener is the backstop for a link added to the page after load.
        try {
            if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
                document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', decorateAll);
            } else {
                decorateAll();
            }
            document.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
                try {
                    decorate(event.target && event.target.closest && event.target.closest('a[href]'));
                } catch (e) { /* no-op */ }
            }, true);
        } catch (e) { /* no-op */ }
    })();
</script>
